<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<CycloneDDS>
  <Domain>
    <General>
      <!-- For the robot (Galactic, Cyclone DDS 0.22.6) ONLY - mars
           (Jazzy, newer Cyclone DDS) uses cyclonedds_jazzy.xml instead.

           spdp, not false, and NO 127.0.0.1 peer: unlike Jazzy's Cyclone
           DDS, adding 127.0.0.1 as an explicit static peer on this older
           Galactic build made same-host discovery WORSE, not better -
           confirmed: controller spawners went from occasionally succeeding
           to failing 100% of the time (5/5 retries) with "empty node name
           returned by the RMW layer" once 127.0.0.1 was added. Likely this
           version's peer-discovery code doesn't handle a self-referential
           unicast peer cleanly. spdp (permit multicast for SPDP discovery
           only, never for data) is the best known option here: it isn't
           fully reliable for same-host discovery either (this Galactic
           Cyclone DDS build has a documented, unfixed upstream discovery-
           timing race - see the retrying_spawner wrapper in
           real_robot_hardware.launch.py, which is the actual mitigation),
           but it's strictly better than 127.0.0.1 here. Cross-host
           discovery (mars<->robot) still goes through the static Peers
           list below regardless - the campus Wi-Fi blocks multicast for
           that regardless of this setting. -->
      <AllowMulticast>spdp</AllowMulticast>
    </General>
    <Discovery>
      <Peers>
        <Peer address="172.30.11.42"/>
        <Peer address="172.27.80.139"/>
      </Peers>
    </Discovery>
    <Internal>
      <!-- KEEP EVERY UDP DATAGRAM UNDER THE ETHERNET MTU (2026-07-26).
           Must match cyclonedds_jazzy.xml, since these settings govern the
           wire format and both ends of the link have to agree.

           Measured, not inferred. In a single pick_place.py run, with the
           robot side log confirming what did and did not arrive:

             gripper close/open   1 waypoint    158 B   DELIVERED
             go_home              2 waypoints   358 B   DELIVERED
             pre-grasp           21 waypoints  1802 B   NEVER ARRIVED (3 of
                                                        3 attempts, fresh
                                                        ActionClient each)
             go_home (final)      2 waypoints   358 B   DELIVERED

           The final 358 B goal was delivered seconds AFTER the three 1802 B
           failures, so the link was healthy throughout and nothing was
           unmatched or degraded. The only variable that predicts delivery
           is whether the serialized message fits in one datagram: 1802 B
           exceeds the 1500 B Ethernet MTU (1472 B of UDP payload) and so
           requires IP level fragmentation, which this campus Wi-Fi link
           silently drops, for the same reason it blocks multicast.

           This also explains, with no extra hypotheses:
             1. the "3-goal wall". Goals 1 to 3 were always go_home plus two
                gripper moves (all small) and goal 4 was always the first
                LARGE one. It was never a count. The WhcHigh=500kB watermark
                previously set here was aimed at that phantom, so it is gone.
             2. `ros2 control list_controllers` timing out cross-machine
                while working locally (Session 1, Problem 6). Its response
                carries every controller's name, type and interface list,
                comfortably over the MTU.
             3. why /joint_states streams flawlessly at ~100 Hz: tiny.

           Cyclone already fragments at FragmentSize (default 1344 B), but
           MaxMessageSize (default 14720 B) then lets it pack several
           fragments back into ONE oversized datagram, which is what
           reintroduces IP fragmentation. Capping MaxMessageSize below the
           MTU forces one sub-MTU datagram per RTPS message.

           NOTE FOR ANYONE REVISITING: commit 204f828 tried FragmentSize and
           MaxMessageSize at 65500B and was reverted (798955c). That was the
           right parameter in the WRONG DIRECTION, because raising
           MaxMessageSize makes Cyclone build BIGGER datagrams and fragment
           harder at the IP layer. Do not raise these. -->
      <MaxMessageSize>1400B</MaxMessageSize>
      <FragmentSize>1300B</FragmentSize>
    </Internal>
  </Domain>
</CycloneDDS>
